PAD is about {app:tag 'tags'/}

<p>
  A single {app:tag 'tag', single/} or 
an open/close combination {app:tag 'tag'/} .... {app:tag 'tag', close/}

  <p>
    A single tag is closed with a trailing <b>/</b> character. A tag written without it opens a pair,
and its closing tag has to follow: under the strict syntax check a tag that has neither is an
error - <code>the pair {pad:open/}pad:echo{pad:close/} never closes - a single tag is written {pad:open/}pad:echo .../{pad:close/}</code> -
and the lenient walk reads it as a single tag. The slash may stand in front of an opening pipe
as well: <code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $x /| upper{pad:close/}</code>. The variable forms - <code>{pad:open/}$x{pad:close/}</code>,
<code>{pad:open/}!x{pad:close/}</code>, <code>{pad:open/}#x{pad:close/}</code> ... - are never pairs and take no slash.

<br><br>
